// Practice quiz: Functions and arguments
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/python-fundamentals/functions-and-arguments

::o1 default value::def fee(amount, rate\=0.1)\: return amount * rate. What does fee(200) return? {
~TypeError\: missing argument rate #Not quite. rate has a default, so the call may leave it out.
=20.0 #Correct. rate is left out, so it takes its default 0.1, and 200 * 0.1 is 20.0.
~200 #Not quite. The default rate is still applied; nothing skips the multiplication.
~None #Not quite. The function returns the product; it does not return None.
}

::o1 keyword order::def move(x, y, speed\=1). Which call passes x\=3, y\=4 and speed\=2 correctly? {
=move(speed\=2, y\=4, x\=3) #Correct. Keyword arguments are matched by name, so any order works.
~move(speed\=2, 3, 4) #Not quite. A positional argument after a keyword argument is a SyntaxError.
~move(3, 4, x\=3, speed\=2) #Not quite. x would get two values, so Python raises TypeError.
~move(2, 4, 3) #Not quite. By position this makes x\=2, y\=4 and speed\=3.
}

::o1 required parameter::def connect(host, port\=5432). What happens with connect(port\=5433)? {
~host becomes None #Not quite. Python never invents a value for a parameter without a default.
~host becomes 5433 #Not quite. port\=5433 is matched by name to port, not to host.
=TypeError\: host is missing #Correct. host has no default, so every call must supply it, by position or by name.
~It works, because port is given by keyword #Not quite. Keyword arguments do not excuse a missing required parameter.
}

::o2 collecting extras::def total(*nums)\: return sum(nums). What is nums inside total(1, 2, 3)? {
~[1, 2, 3], a list #Not quite. The starred parameter always receives a tuple, not a list.
~1, only the first argument #Not quite. The star collects all of them.
~6 #Not quite. nums holds the arguments; sum(nums) computes 6.
=(1, 2, 3), a tuple #Correct. *nums gathers every extra positional argument into a tuple.
}

::o2 unpacking a dict::def show(name, role)\: ... and person \= dict(name\="Ana", role\="dev"). Which call passes name\="Ana" and role\="dev"? {
~show(person) #Not quite. The whole dict arrives as name, and role is missing, so Python raises TypeError.
~show(*person) #Not quite. One star spreads the dict's keys, so name gets "name" and role gets "role".
=show(**person) #Correct. ** spreads the dict into keyword arguments whose names match the parameters.
~show(&person) #Not quite. & is not an unpacking operator in Python.
}

::o2 star in a call::point \= [5, 7]. Which call is the same as move(5, 7)? {
=move(*point) #Correct. * in a call spreads the list into separate positional arguments.
~move(point) #Not quite. The list arrives as a single argument.
~move(**point) #Not quite. ** needs a mapping such as a dict, not a list.
~move(point[]) #Not quite. This is a SyntaxError.
}

::o3 hints at run time::def area(w\: float, h\: float) has a float return annotation and returns w * h. What does area("x", 3) do? {
~Raises TypeError because w must be float #Not quite. Python does not check hints when the function runs.
~Converts "x" to a float first #Not quite. Hints never convert values.
~Fails when the def is executed #Not quite. The annotated def is valid and runs normally.
=Returns 'xxx' #Correct. Hints are not enforced, and a str times an int repeats the string.
}

::o3 purpose of hints::Why add type hints to a Python function if Python does not enforce them? {
~They make the function run faster #Not quite. The interpreter does not use hints to speed up the call.
=They document intent and let tools such as type checkers and editors find mismatches before you run the code #Correct. The typing documentation says the runtime does not enforce annotations; third-party tools use them.
~They stop wrong types at run time #Not quite. Nothing is checked at run time.
~They are required for keyword arguments #Not quite. Keyword arguments work with or without hints.
}
